Chapter: IntOps

 Integer primitives safe for real-time contexts. Every function is
 punctual: no heap, no recursion, bounded instruction count.

 To int-abs gives the absolute value of an integer.
 To int-sign gives -1, 0, or 1 indicating the sign of an integer.
 To int-min gives the smaller of two integers.
 To int-max gives the larger of two integers.
 To int-clamp gives an integer constrained to bounds.
 To is-even gives True when the integer is divisible by two.
 To is-odd gives True when the integer is not divisible by two.
 To is-positive gives True when the integer is greater than zero.
 To is-negative gives True when the integer is less than zero.
 To is-zero gives True when the integer is zero.

 We say:

Section: Absolute Value and Sign

  punctual int-abs : Integer -> Integer
  int-abs (n) = if n < 0 then 0 - n else n

  punctual int-sign : Integer -> Integer
  int-sign (n) = if n < 0 then 0 - 1 else if n > 0 then 1 else 0

Section: Comparison

  punctual int-min : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  int-min (a) (b) = if a < b then a else b

  punctual int-max : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  int-max (a) (b) = if a > b then a else b

  punctual int-clamp : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  int-clamp (lo) (hi) (v) = if v < lo then lo else if v > hi then hi else v

Section: Predicates

  punctual is-even : Integer -> Boolean
  is-even (n) = bit-and n 1 == 0

  punctual is-odd : Integer -> Boolean
  is-odd (n) = bit-and n 1 == 1

  punctual is-positive : Integer -> Boolean
  is-positive (n) = n > 0

  punctual is-negative : Integer -> Boolean
  is-negative (n) = n < 0

  punctual is-zero : Integer -> Boolean
  is-zero (n) = n == 0

